<aside class="notes"><p>We have people here at very different stages and in very different roles—medical students, trainees, junior and senior faculty, and colleagues in industry. We’re coming to this conversation for different reasons. Some are learning how to contribute to their first paper. Others are leading collaborations or managing publication programs. What we need from this discussion will differ.</p><p>But we often work together on the same science. And we may bring different expectations about what earns authorship, who should be invited to contribute, and when those conversations should happen. Those differences can stay invisible until a manuscript is nearly finished.</p><p>One of the most valuable things we can do is hear how our colleagues have handled these situations. It may be someone whose judgment you respect, someone you look up to, or someone at a similar stage who has faced the same problem. Hearing what they did, what was difficult, and how they reached a decision gives you something to draw on when it happens to you.</p><p>I hope we leave with a useful framework—earlier, explicitly, transparently, and revisited—and with guidance, reassurance, and confidence from those shared experiences. The next time an invitation arrives or an authorship conversation feels uncomfortable, you may remember something a colleague said today and feel better equipped to decide what to ask or how to respond.</p></aside>

