## A process heuristic the room can use {#process .aoa-slide}

<div class="aoa-shell aoa-has-takeaway">
<div class="aoa-topbar">
<div class="aoa-section-label">
<p>DISCUSSION</p>
</div>
<div class="aoa-heading" role="heading" aria-level="2">
<p>A process heuristic the room can use</p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="aoa-content">
<div class="process-grid">
<div class="process-card fragment fade-in" data-fragment-index="0">
<div class="process-number">
<p>01</p>
</div>
<div class="process-title">
<p>Earlier</p>
</div>
<p>Discuss the process before expectations harden and before the work is nearly complete.</p>
</div>
<div class="process-card fragment fade-in" data-fragment-index="1">
<div class="process-number">
<p>02</p>
</div>
<div class="process-title">
<p>Explicitly</p>
</div>
<p>State what contributions are expected and what authorship requires.</p>
</div>
<div class="process-card fragment fade-in" data-fragment-index="2">
<div class="process-number">
<p>03</p>
</div>
<div class="process-title">
<p>Transparently</p>
</div>
<p>Make the basis for decisions visible enough that people can understand the outcome.</p>
</div>
<div class="process-card fragment fade-in" data-fragment-index="3">
<div class="process-number">
<p>04</p>
</div>
<div class="process-title">
<p>Revisited</p>
</div>
<p>Update the plan as actual contributions evolve.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="aoa-bottom-line fragment fade-in" data-fragment-index="4">
<p>Decide the process early — not necessarily the final byline.</p>
</div>
</div>

<aside class="notes"><p>One way to organize what we have heard is earlier: start the conversation while there is still room for people to shape the work.</p><p>Explicitly: say what contribution is expected and what the pathway involves.</p><p>Transparently: make the reasoning understandable to the people affected by the decision.</p><p>And revisited: allow the plan to change as the actual work becomes clearer.</p><p>Those ideas can give us a practical framework. Alongside them, we now have examples of how colleagues have approached these decisions. Both can help when we face a situation that feels uncertain or difficult.</p></aside>

