<div class="aoa-shell"><div class="aoa-topbar"><div class="aoa-section-label">CASE 04 · EDITOR PERSPECTIVE · ADE ADAMSON</div><div class="aoa-heading">Ade, what can an editor actually see?</div></div><div class="aoa-content"><div class="revamp-editor"><div class="revamp-editor-item fragment fade-in" data-fragment-index="0"><div class="revamp-number">01</div><div><div class="revamp-item-title">How clear is the contribution story?</div><div class="revamp-item-copy">What can—and can’t—you infer from the submission?</div></div></div><div class="revamp-editor-item fragment fade-in" data-fragment-index="1"><div class="revamp-number">02</div><div><div class="revamp-item-title">What would raise concern?</div><div class="revamp-item-copy">When would the author list prompt a closer look?</div></div></div><div class="revamp-editor-item fragment fade-in" data-fragment-index="2"><div class="revamp-number">03</div><div><div class="revamp-item-title">How do you ask for clarification?</div><div class="revamp-item-copy">What explanation or documentation would help?</div></div></div></div></div></div>

<aside class="notes"><p>Ade, how clear is that contribution story from the editor’s desk? What can you actually infer from the manuscript, author list, and contribution statement?</p><p>How much concern does this create in practice? What would make you pause and take a closer look at whether the byline represents the work?</p><p>When you have needed clarification, how have you asked for it? What would you want the authors to explain about the panel, the writing group, and the responsibilities of the people named as authors?</p></aside>

## What does a meaningful path look like? {#module-1-path-problem .aoa-slide data-aoa-section="case-1"}

<div class="aoa-shell"><div class="aoa-topbar"><div class="aoa-section-label">CASE 01 · OPTIONAL BACKUP</div><div class="aoa-heading">What does a meaningful path look like?</div></div><div class="aoa-content"><div class="revamp-agenda"><div class="revamp-agenda-item fragment fade-in" data-fragment-index="0"><div class="revamp-number">01</div><div><div class="revamp-item-title">The manuscript is already finished.</div><div class="revamp-item-copy">“Please review and approve.”</div></div></div><div class="revamp-agenda-item fragment fade-in" data-fragment-index="1"><div class="revamp-number">02</div><div><div class="revamp-item-title">But what can the contributor still shape?</div><div class="revamp-item-copy">The analysis? The interpretation? The scientific story?</div></div></div><div class="revamp-agenda-item fragment fade-in" data-fragment-index="2"><div class="revamp-number">03</div><div><div class="revamp-item-title">An invitation needs room for contribution.</div><div class="revamp-item-copy">What would make this a meaningful path to qualify?</div></div></div></div></div></div>

<aside class="notes"><p>A familiar version of the problem is that the manuscript arrives essentially finished, with a request to review and approve it.</p><p>Substantive revision can still matter. But what room have we left for the contributor to question the analysis, change the interpretation, or reshape the story?</p><p>What would make that invitation a real opportunity to contribute?</p></aside>

## Office hours {#module-1-office-hours .aoa-slide data-aoa-section="case-1"}

<div class="aoa-shell"><div class="aoa-topbar"><div class="aoa-section-label">CASE 01 · OPTIONAL BACKUP</div><div class="aoa-heading">Office hours</div></div><div class="aoa-content"><div class="revamp-agenda"><div class="revamp-agenda-item fragment fade-in" data-fragment-index="0"><div class="revamp-number">01</div><div><div class="revamp-item-title">Invite clinicians into the project.</div><div class="revamp-item-copy">Start with their connection to the patient cohort.</div></div></div><div class="revamp-agenda-item fragment fade-in" data-fragment-index="1"><div class="revamp-number">02</div><div><div class="revamp-item-title">Make the methods visible.</div><div class="revamp-item-copy">Patient identification · abstraction · analysis · preliminary findings</div></div></div><div class="revamp-agenda-item fragment fade-in" data-fragment-index="2"><div class="revamp-number">03</div><div><div class="revamp-item-title">Create a place to contribute.</div><div class="revamp-item-copy">Brief office hours for questions, interpretation, and refinement.</div></div></div><div class="revamp-agenda-item fragment fade-in" data-fragment-index="3"><div class="revamp-number">04</div><div><div class="revamp-item-title">Let people choose to engage.</div><div class="revamp-item-copy">Offer several sessions and a clear, low-pressure invitation.</div></div></div></div><div class="case-one-principle fragment fade-in" data-fragment-index="4">An opportunity to contribute. Authorship still requires the full criteria.</div></div></div>

<aside class="notes"><p>Here is one approach we tried. We identified potential contributors through their involvement in patient care and invited them into a discussion of the project.</p><p>We explained how patients were identified, how abstraction and analysis were performed, and what the preliminary findings showed.</p><p>The office hours gave people a place to ask questions, engage with the data, strengthen the interpretation, and identify what might need refinement.</p><p>We offered several dates and made it easy to choose whether the project fit their interests and bandwidth.</p><p>The session was an entry point to engagement, not an authorship credential by itself. What would you keep or change about that approach?</p></aside>

